    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "narrator",
        "speaker": "Narrator",
        "sourceStart": 0,
        "sourceEnd": 542,
        "text": "Good morning, everyone. Today I want to talk about a question that comes up whenever a study works well in one place: can we use it somewhere else? This is the idea of transferability. I'm Dr. Lin, and with me is my colleague Dr. Patel, who will add some comments. Let's start with a simple definition. Transferability means the degree to which the results of a study can be reasonably applied to a different group, setting, or time. It is not the same as copying a method. It is about judging whether the findings still make sense elsewhere.",
        "boundary": "paragraph",
        "voice": "en-US-EmmaNeural",
        "textHash": "9f55a3e2ab81203fdd772f66c68f4abd7cc72e271b6cc1af63b7d79e608c422d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "dr patel",
        "speaker": "Dr. Patel",
        "sourceStart": 555,
        "sourceEnd": 832,
        "text": "May I add something here? Many people confuse transferability with generalization. Generalization is about how widely results hold for a larger population. Transferability is more practical: it asks whether a specific new context is similar enough for the results to be useful.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "58db649d0f8a02326a97743c8dba0349b3aad856b6d0f8da122016695c45518b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "dr lin",
        "speaker": "Dr. Lin",
        "sourceStart": 843,
        "sourceEnd": 1361,
        "text": "Exactly. So how do we assess it? I suggest four checks. First, look at the participants. Were they similar to the people in the new setting in age, background, and needs? Second, look at the conditions. Did the original study depend on special resources, such as small classes or extra staff? Third, look at the outcome measures. Did the study measure the same thing that the new setting cares about? Fourth, look at the time. Results from ten years ago may not transfer to today, because the context may have changed.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "8a4162016cd4d5c3a5e18d79f74242c32a85b627e0eb3c621a58abe73d16944f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "dr patel",
        "speaker": "Dr. Patel",
        "sourceStart": 1374,
        "sourceEnd": 1748,
        "text": "I'd like to give an example. A reading program was tested in three urban schools with class sizes of about eighteen students. It improved reading scores after twelve weeks. A rural school with classes of thirty-five wants to adopt it. The method might still work, but the class size is a different condition. So we cannot assume the same result. We need a small trial first.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "b406c90c516674ee4b2cdd758d0ce8a35acf2069f385411239940db25bff1011",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "dr lin",
        "speaker": "Dr. Lin",
        "sourceStart": 1759,
        "sourceEnd": 2028,
        "text": "That's a good point. Notice that Dr. Patel did not say the program would fail. She said we cannot assume the same result. That is an important difference. Transferability is a judgment, not a yes-or-no answer. Sometimes we say 'promising but unproven' in a new context.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "7f1b196d113112742765083a4b0b10c95339e105d9341fced389f8df2ac4c0a8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "dr patel",
        "speaker": "Dr. Patel",
        "sourceStart": 2041,
        "sourceEnd": 2478,
        "text": "Another common mistake is to focus only on the average result. A study may report an average improvement of five points, but the improvement may be uneven. Some students gained a lot; others gained nothing. When you transfer the program, you need to ask who benefited most. If the new group looks like the low-gain group, the average may mislead you. This is why we should examine subgroup data when available, not just the overall mean.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-MichelleNeural",
        "textHash": "c6807e788d744cb36e46439d95cddd6f958798718b24fb58aae3d13d0181c19f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "dr lin",
        "speaker": "Dr. Lin",
        "sourceStart": 2489,
        "sourceEnd": 2894,
        "text": "Right. So a careful assessment asks: who benefited, under what conditions, and why? If we can answer those questions, we can make a reasoned decision. If we cannot, we should be cautious. Now, let me summarize the main message. Transferability is about fit, not about copying. It requires evidence from the new setting, even if that evidence is small. A pilot study is often the best next step. Thank you.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-ChristopherNeural",
        "textHash": "1fa38ccab822dba304c5db13d9641cd8acba28d0177eb3120be2f074f1a0f410",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
